.TH GIT-PRETEND 1 "2026-11-24" "git-pretend 0.1.0" "Git Manual"
.SH NAME
git-pretend \- review a range of commits as if you had just written it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git pretend
.I commit
.br
.B git pretend
.I old new
.br
.B git pretend
.IB old .. new
.br
.B git pretend
.IB base ... new
.br
.B git pretend
.RB [ pick
.RI [ log-args ]]
.br
.B git pretend status
.RB [ \-\-short | \-\-porcelain ]
.br
.B git pretend done
.SH DESCRIPTION
While pretending, HEAD is detached at the older commit and the working tree holds the newer
commit's files as uncommitted, unstaged changes, with files added in the range marked
intent-to-add. Every tool that understands "my changes" (git status, git diff, editor gutters,
LSP, test runners, coding agents) then works on the range.
.PP
Pretending is for reading.
.B git pretend done
discards edits made while pretending, saving them first as a patch in
.IR $GIT_DIR/pretend-discarded/ ,
and switches back to the branch or commit you started on. Untracked files are never deleted;
the ones created while pretending are listed.
.PP
State is kept in
.IR $GIT_DIR/pretend/ ,
per worktree, so it survives closing your shell or editor.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.I commit
Pretend you wrote
.IR commit :
HEAD at its parent, working tree at
.IR commit .
.TP
.I old new\fR, \fIold\fB..\fInew
Pretend you wrote everything from
.I old
to
.IR new .
An empty side of
.B ..
means HEAD.
.TP
.IB base ... new
Start from the merge base of
.I base
and
.IR new ,
as a pull request of
.I new
into
.I base
would show it.
.B git pretend main...
reviews the current branch.
.TP
.BR pick " [\fIlog-args\fR]"
Choose commits with fzf from
.B git log
.IR log-args .
Enter picks one commit; Tab two commits, then Enter, picks the range between them.
.B git pretend
with no arguments does the same when run in a terminal, or shows the status while pretending.
.TP
.BR status " [" \-\-short | \-\-porcelain ]
Show what you are pretending. Exits 0 while pretending and 1 otherwise. With
.BR \-\-short ,
prints just the branch you will go back to (or the commit, if you started detached).
While pretending HEAD is detached, so prompts can show this instead of a commit hash. With
.BR \-\-porcelain ,
prints the state as lines of
.B key value
.RB ( version ,
.BR prev-ref ,
.BR prev-commit ,
.BR base ,
.BR target ),
or nothing.
.TP
.B done
Discard the pretend changes and go back. If HEAD was moved since pretending started, the state is
cleared and nothing else is touched.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git-reset (1),
.BR git-switch (1),
.BR fzf (1)
.PP
https://github.com/alex-k03/git-pretend
